WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.617
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:16.784
Unit sixteen: Small Business Growth. Today you will express purpose with in order to, so as to and so that.

4
00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:19.887
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.066
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.266
Local entrepreneur.

7
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.560
Local entrepreneur means a business owner who starts a company in their own area.

8
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.405
A local entrepreneur opened a bakery.

9
00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:37.924
Your turn. Say: Local entrepreneur.

10
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:42.499
Local entrepreneur.

11
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.379
Business loan.

12
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:48.660
Business loan means money borrowed to start or grow a company.

13
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.331
A business loan paid for the new oven.

14
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.499
Your turn. Say: Business loan.

15
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:58.532
Business loan.

16
00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:00.540
Customer base.

17
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.931
Customer base means the regular customers of a business.

18
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:07.876
The shop built a loyal customer base.

19
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:11.182
Your turn. Say: Customer base.

20
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:15.406
Customer base.

21
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.325
Cash flow.

22
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:21.350
Cash flow means the money moving into and out of a business.

23
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.576
Poor cash flow can close a healthy business.

24
00:01:25.183 --> 00:01:27.917
Your turn. Say: Cash flow.

25
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:31.837
Cash flow.

26
00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:34.026
Marketing budget.

27
00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:38.897
Marketing budget means the money a company plans to spend on promotion.

28
00:01:39.483 --> 00:01:42.214
A small marketing budget needs clever ideas.

29
00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:45.875
Your turn. Say: Marketing budget.

30
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:50.292
Marketing budget.

31
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.272
Hiring decision.

32
00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:57.280
Hiring decision means a choice about whether to employ a new worker.

33
00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.730
Every hiring decision affects costs.

34
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:04.190
Your turn. Say: Hiring decision.

35
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:08.418
Hiring decision.

36
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:10.231
Scaling up.

37
00:02:11.217 --> 00:02:13.611
Scaling up means making a business bigger.

38
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:16.936
Scaling up requires more staff and space.

39
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.237
Your turn. Say: Scaling up.

40
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.264
Scaling up.

41
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:26.540
Community support.

42
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.349
Community support means help from the people who live near a business.

43
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.321
Community support kept the shop open.

44
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:37.499
Your turn. Say: Community support.

45
00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:41.961
Community support.

46
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:46.452
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.317 --> 00:02:53.150
Visal, a profile says 'She took a business loan in order to buy a second oven.' Why 'in order to'?

48
00:02:54.250 --> 00:03:01.047
It states purpose. 'To' alone also works, but 'in order to' and 'so as to' sound more formal.

49
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.314
And 'so that'?

50
00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:12.003
Use so that when the subject changes or with a modal: 'She kept records so that the bank could trust her cash flow.'

51
00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:15.302
How does the community help?

52
00:03:16.417 --> 00:03:25.071
Neighbours shop locally so that small firms can survive. Their support also lets a business spend its marketing budget wisely.

53
00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:27.954
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.817 --> 00:03:33.603
Grammar focus: Purpose clauses: in order to and so as to and so that.

55
00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:35.078
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.783 --> 00:03:38.178
She took a loan in order to buy a second oven.

57
00:03:39.083 --> 00:03:41.745
He hired a cook so as to serve more customers.

58
00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.857
They kept records so that the bank could check their cash flow.

59
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:54.958
In order to / so as to + base verb (same subject). So that + clause (often with can, could, will). Negative: so as not to.

60
00:03:55.717 --> 00:03:59.205
Be careful! Don't say: She saved so that to buy a shop.

61
00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:04.141
Say: She saved so that she could buy a shop.

62
00:04:04.441 --> 00:04:06.000
So that needs a clause.

63
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.150
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.967 --> 00:04:11.328
A Bakery Grows.

65
00:04:11.628 --> 00:04:15.253
A local entrepreneur opened a small bakery with her own savings.

66
00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:21.204
She took a business loan in order to buy a second oven and so as to serve more customers.

67
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.313
She watched her cash flow carefully so that she could always pay suppliers.

68
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.109
Because neighbours bought bread every day, her customer base grew, and she made a hiring decision.

69
00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.796
Community support helped her begin scaling up.

70
00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.820
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:41.683 --> 00:04:43.903
She took a loan blank buy a second oven.

72
00:04:44.203 --> 00:04:47.892
A, in order to. B, in order for. C, for.

73
00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:53.375
The answer is A: in order to.

74
00:04:54.517 --> 00:04:57.631
The money moving into and out of a business is cash blank.

75
00:04:57.931 --> 00:05:01.097
A, flow. B, base. C, budget.

76
00:05:04.633 --> 00:05:06.331
The answer is A: flow.

77
00:05:07.483 --> 00:05:09.494
Making a business bigger is blank.

78
00:05:09.794 --> 00:05:13.854
A, scaling up. B, hiring. C, supporting.

79
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.458
The answer is A: scaling up.

80
00:05:20.617 --> 00:05:22.349
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:22.649 --> 00:05:30.366
A, She saved so that she can buy a shop.. B, She saved so that she could buy a shop.. C, She saved so that to buy a shop..

82
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.235
The answer is B: She saved so that she could buy a shop..

83
00:05:38.383 --> 00:05:42.426
According to the reading, her customer base grew because neighbours bought bread…

84
00:05:42.726 --> 00:05:46.670
A, every day. B, once a year. C, online.

85
00:05:50.233 --> 00:05:52.570
The answer is A: every day.

86
00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:56.674
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:57.517 --> 00:06:01.258
How can a community help a small local business succeed?

88
00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.376
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:11.676 --> 00:06:36.219
A community can help by buying from local shops regularly, which gives a small business steady income. People can also recommend it to friends so that its customer base grows. In addition, local officials can offer training or simple loans so that owners can expand. Community support is often the difference between failure and success.

90
00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:38.228
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:39.117 --> 00:06:45.055
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and purpose clauses: in order to and so as to and so that.

92
00:06:45.355 --> 00:06:49.865
Today's value is community spirit. Help and care for people in your town.

93
00:06:50.165 --> 00:06:53.026
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:53.326 --> 00:06:56.779
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

